# CONTAINMENT of the Shibboleth repository declared in pom.xml: only these two
# groupIds may ever be requested from it. Generated, not guessed, with
# -Daether.remoteRepositoryFilter.groupId.record=true.
-Daether.remoteRepositoryFilter.groupId=true
-Daether.remoteRepositoryFilter.groupId.basedir=${session.rootDirectory}/.mvn/rrf/

# CHECKSUM VERIFICATION for those same artifacts, closing the trust-on-first-use
# gap the filter leaves open: every artifact resolved from shibboleth-releases is
# checked against .mvn/checksums/checksums-shibboleth-releases.sha1, which is
# committed and reviewed. A repository (or a MITM) that swaps a pinned jar under
# us now fails the build instead of shipping.
#
# Resolver's own post-processor, so no plugin and nothing extra to download. It is
# origin-aware: the file name carries the repository id, the enhanced local
# repository manager records each artifact's origin in _remote.repositories, and
# verification therefore also fires on cache hits — `./mvnw -o` stays fully
# offline AND fully verified.
#
# Central is deliberately NOT covered (no checksums-central.sha1), and
# failIfMissing stays at its default of false: pinning every transitive of the
# whole build would turn each dependency bump into a re-record and each
# dynamically resolved plugin into a build break. Central is the repository we are
# not worried about; Shibboleth is the one this exists for.
#
# After bumping ${opensaml.version} the recorded set MUST be regenerated, or the
# new jars resolve unverified:
#   ./mvnw -Daether.artifactResolver.postProcessor.trustedChecksums.record=true \
#          dependency:resolve
#   rm .mvn/checksums/checksums-central.sha1   # recording writes this too
-Daether.artifactResolver.postProcessor.trustedChecksums=true
-Daether.trustedChecksumsSource.summaryFile=true
-Daether.trustedChecksumsSource.summaryFile.basedir=${session.rootDirectory}/.mvn/checksums
